LN(1)                          P32W Unix                           LN(1)

NAME
       ln - make links to files

SYNOPSIS
       ln [-f] old new
       ln [-f] file ... directory

DESCRIPTION
       ln makes new another name for the existing file old: a hard
       link, a second directory entry for the same inode. Both
       names are equal; the file's link count (ls -l) goes up by
       one, and the file lasts until every name is removed. If the
       last argument is a directory, each file is linked into it
       under its last path component.

       new must not exist (unless -f is given), its directory must
       be writable, and it must be on the same filesystem as old
       (else "cross-device link": copy the file with cp(1)
       instead). Directories are not linked.

OPTIONS
       -f     remove an existing new first. Not if it is a
              directory, the same file as old, or on another
              filesystem; then nothing is removed.
       --     ends the options.

EXAMPLES
       ln /usr/ken/hello.c /usr/guest/hello.c
       ln prog prog.old
       ln *.h /usr/include           several into a directory

EXIT STATUS
       0 if every link was made; 1 if not (ln: name: and the
       reason, such as file exists or the same file), or if there
       are more than two arguments and the last is not a directory;
       2 for a usage error (fewer than two arguments, an unknown
       option).

SEE ALSO
       rm(1), mv(1), cp(1), ls(1), link(2)

BUGS
       There are no symbolic links and no -s.
